  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nAnother complex convective forecast will occur through the weekend. \nAn MCS has been developing in Illinois and Missouri. It is not clear \nhow this evolves as it moves east. There may be weakening into the \nmorning, but then the MCV associated with it combined with diurnal \nheating may lead to a line of storms that will sweep across the\nregion this afternoon, that would bring a damaging wind threat.\nHowever, a number of convective allowing models show little to no \nintensification as this moves across the area.\n\nAnother disturbance will quickly follow this evening into the\novernight hours. New development is forecast to occur as this moves\nacross the area. A modest westerly low level jet will be nosing into\nthe area. The environment will still be characterized by anomalously\nhigh precipitable water and a very deep warm cloud layer. So\nextremely efficient rainfall rates are expected. This could lead to\nflash flooding, especially if any areas get repeated rounds of\nstorms. In addition, there may be a weak surface low that will\nback winds. So there is a non-zero tornadic threat.\n\nMore storms are possible on Sunday ahead of an approaching cold\nfront. The atmosphere will be quite unstable, so storms could become\nquite robust leading to a damaging wind threat.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nLooks like there may be a fairly decent north-south temperature\ngradient across the area which could end up being enhanced depending\non how convection evolves. Areas near and south of the Ohio River\nhave the lower chance of storms and temperatures should approach 90.\nCombined with mid 70s dew points, the heat index will be near 100 in\nthat part of the area. Air temperatures and heat indices may be just\na bit higher area wide on Sunday.\n\nA cold front will move through the region late Sunday and Sunday\nnight. In the wake of the front, temperatures will fall to or\nslightly below normal. Drier air may lag slightly, but by Tuesday it\nshould be be noticeably less humid.",
